---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import DiagramTrack from '@dunn.dev/reference/chrome/DiagramTrack.astro';
import Split from '@dunn.dev/reference/chrome/Split.astro';
import trustBoundary from '../../public/diagrams/trust-boundary.svg?raw';
import oidcLogin from '../../public/diagrams/oidc-login-path.svg?raw';
import forwardAuth from '../../public/diagrams/forward-auth-request-path.svg?raw';
import groupBinding from '../../public/diagrams/group-binding-decision.svg?raw';
import sessionRevocation from '../../public/diagrams/session-revocation.svg?raw';
import firstCredential from '../../public/diagrams/first-credential.svg?raw';

const label = (svg: string, text: string) =>
  svg.replace('<svg ', `<svg aria-label="${text.replace(/"/g, '&quot;')}" `);
---
<Layout title="Architecture" description="How Kratos, Hydra, the portal, and the edge fit together in Othala, told through six diagrams: trust boundary, OIDC login, forward-auth, group binding, session revocation, and the first credential.">
  <header class="page-header">
    <p class="page-eyebrow">Othala</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">Architecture</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">
      Six diagrams: who is inside the trust boundary, how a login becomes a
      token, how an app with no OIDC gets an answer anyway, and what
      happens on sign-out.
    </p>
    <p class="page-lede">
      Then how the first credential gets in the door, and how one group
      becomes two different grants. Each diagram carries the what in its
      caption; the prose beside it is the why.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>One trust domain</h2>
    <Split>
      <p>
        Everything downstream of a login trusts whatever token or session
        Othala hands it, so the first question is what can reach the two Ory
        admin APIs at all. The answer is one process. The portal runs a
        public listener the edge reaches and a second, internal-network
        admin listener the edge never touches, so every admin-API caller
        stays inside one trust domain instead of becoming a
        service-to-service authorization problem of its own.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>ARCHITECTURE.md</code> § Trust boundary has the enforcement;
        the <a href="/guides/hardening/">Hardening</a> guide's Admin-API
        isolation section walks the control end to end.
      </p>
      <figure class="diagram" slot="aside">
        <Fragment set:html={label(trustBoundary, 'A browser reaches one hostname at the edge proxy, which terminates TLS. Inside one trust domain the admin APIs never leave: the app runs a public listener the edge reaches and an admin listener the edge never touches; Kratos and Hydra each expose a public API to the edge, while the app alone calls their admin APIs. Kratos and Hydra each keep a separate Postgres database.')} />
        <figcaption class="diagram-caption">The network sees only the edge; the admin APIs are not behind it</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </Split>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <DiagramTrack caption="One code exchange, four parties, one live Kratos session underneath it">
      <h2>How a login becomes a token</h2>
      <p data-step="1">
        A relying party, here Grafana, redirects the browser to Hydra. Hydra
        ships no login UI of its own, so it opens a login challenge on the
        portal, and with no session yet the portal sends the browser to
        Kratos.
      </p>
      <p data-step="2">
        Kratos runs the passkey ceremony, sets the session cookie and resumes
        the authorize request. A remembered subject skips the passkey but
        never the gate: Hydra's memory of a past login is never trusted
        without a live Kratos session behind it.
      </p>
      <p data-step="3">
        At consent the portal gates again, then maps groups to the claims the
        token carries. Hydra hands Grafana a code to exchange server-side.
        The <a href="/guides/per-app-oidc/">Per-app OIDC</a> guide walks one
        relying party through it; <code>ARCHITECTURE.md</code> § The OIDC
        path has the sequencing.
      </p>
      <Fragment slot="figure" set:html={label(oidcLogin, 'A sequence across four lifelines: browser, Hydra, the Othala portal and Kratos, for an example relying party, Grafana. The browser is redirected to Hydra, which opens a login challenge on the portal; with no session the portal sends the browser to Kratos, which runs the passkey challenge, sets the session cookie and resumes at Hydra. Hydra opens a consent challenge; the portal gates again, maps groups to claims and accepts. Hydra redirects the browser back to Grafana with a code, exchanged server-side for tokens.')} />
    </DiagramTrack>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <DiagramTrack caption="One sub-request decides the request, before the app ever sees it">
      <h2>The apps that speak no OIDC</h2>
      <p data-step="1">
        Not every app in the house can run an OAuth2 redirect, so the edge
        asks a question instead. Before any proxying, it sends a sub-request
        to the portal carrying the session cookie and a group name that the
        edge's own config sets, never an inbound header.
      </p>
      <p data-step="2">
        The portal verifies the session, then re-resolves the identity
        through the Kratos admin API, because group metadata is not in
        whoami. The gate fails closed at every step: an inactive identity, a
        non-canonical stored group or a missing group each refuse.
      </p>
      <p data-step="3">
        The answer is a status, and each status means one thing. 401 is the
        only denial an edge may turn into a login redirect. 503 means Kratos
        never answered; folding it into 403 would turn an outage into an
        endless bounce. <code>ARCHITECTURE.md</code> § The forward-auth
        request path has the contract.
      </p>
      <Fragment slot="figure" set:html={label(forwardAuth, 'A browser carrying the session cookie reaches the edge, then the service\'s own proxy, where forward_auth runs before any reverse_proxy. The proxy sends a sub-request to the portal with the required group set by the edge config. The portal verifies the session, re-resolves the identity through the Kratos admin API and applies the group gate, failing closed. It answers 200 to proxy through with the identity header set, 401 for no usable session, the one status an edge may turn into a login redirect, 403 for a refusal on the merits, and 503 when Kratos did not answer.')} />
    </DiagramTrack>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <DiagramTrack caption="Hydra remembers; Kratos authenticates, and revoking cuts both">
      <h2>What actually happens on sign-out</h2>
      <p data-step="1">
        Hydra and Kratos each remember something about a session. When Hydra
        says it remembers a subject, the login handler still checks for a
        live Kratos session belonging to that same identity before honouring
        the skip. No match means signing in again.
      </p>
      <p data-step="2">
        Revoking a subject is one in-process fan-out that cuts the Kratos
        sessions and both of Hydra's remembered sessions in a fixed order,
        whatever the trigger. It stops sessions, not credentials, so a
        lost-device eviction deletes the credential first; the
        <a href="/guides/hardening/">Hardening</a> guide has that procedure.
      </p>
      <Fragment slot="figure" set:html={label(sessionRevocation, 'Two panels. Left: when Hydra remembers a subject, the login handler re-verifies a live Kratos session for that identity before honouring the skip; no live match forces a fresh sign-in. Right: one revocation fan-out, triggered by admin force-logout, sign-out-everywhere or deactivation, revokes the Kratos sessions, Hydra\'s login session and Hydra\'s consent session in a fixed order. It cuts sessions and remembered logins, not enrolled credentials.')} />
    </DiagramTrack>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <DiagramTrack caption='Not "no password exists": no password ever transits a channel'>
      <h2>How the first credential gets in the door</h2>
      <p data-step="1">
        There is no self-service registration. An admin creates each
        identity through the admin API with no credentials block at all,
        then mints a recovery link and a one-time code together and hands
        them over on a conversation that already exists with that person.
      </p>
      <p data-step="2">
        The person redeems the code in their own browser, which is where a
        password and a passkey are first set. Neither ever leaves it.
      </p>
      <p data-step="3">
        One branch refuses outright. When the target carries the admin
        marker, recovery is refused before Kratos is ever asked, so an
        admin's own recovery stays at the console.
      </p>
      <Fragment slot="figure" set:html={label(firstCredential, 'An admin creates the identity through the admin API with no credentials block, then mints a one-time recovery link and code together and hands both over on a conversation that already exists with that person. The person\'s own browser is where a password and a passkey are first set. One branch refuses outright: when the target carries the admin marker, recovery is refused before Kratos is asked, console-only.')} />
    </DiagramTrack>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>One group gets you in; the other decides what you can do</h2>
    <Split>
      <p>
        Every manifest binds a group twice, and the two bindings do
        different jobs on purpose. <code>presentation.access</code> is
        entry: hold it and the gate admits you.
        <code>consent.group_role_map</code> is role: it elevates someone
        already admitted and grants nothing on its own, so holding only an
        app's admin group gets a person nowhere.
      </p>
      <p>
        Keeping the two questions on two bindings is what makes an admin
        group safe to have at all. The <a href="/guides/per-app-oidc/">Per-app OIDC</a>
        guide has the manifest shape; <code>ARCHITECTURE.md</code> § The
        access model has the resolution order.
      </p>
      <figure class="diagram" slot="aside">
        <Fragment set:html={label(groupBinding, 'Three columns. The identity holds groups in admin-API-only metadata. The manifest binds them twice: presentation.access is the entry binding, consent.group_role_map is the role binding. The code then decides: the access gate admits on the entry binding, and the role resolves from the highest-ranked match. Holding only the admin group admits nobody, and a group no manifest binds grants nothing.')} />
        <figcaption class="diagram-caption">The identity holds · the manifest binds · the code decides</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </Split>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <p>
      That is the shape of it end to end. <code>ARCHITECTURE.md</code> has
      the rest of the reasoning, <code>DECISIONS.md</code> has the rulings no change may
      reverse, and the <a href="https://gitlab.com/dunn.dev/othala">repository</a>
      has the code and the tests these diagrams are drawn from.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
